{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1792859330192,"sessionID":"ses_e9141cfbdffenLAFgwFuHpfkdd","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_02_GA8rXGGDD5nR3zTKyVHj9207","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"C:\\src\\m0saic-production\\one-a-day\\journal\\2026-10-24\\10-scout.md"},"output":"<path>C:\\src\\m0saic-production\\one-a-day\\journal\\2026-10-24\\10-scout.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Scout — 2026-10-24\n2: \n3: ## Candidates (4, best first)\n4: \n5: ### 1. Orienteering race split-analysis card\n6: - Who: Orienteers. They gather on **Attackpoint** (https://attackpoint.org/ — the sport's\n7:   training-log and race-discussion hub, with a dedicated \"Recent Splits\" section and an\n8:   \"upload results\" flow), on **WinSplits** result pages (obasen.orientering.se), and in club\n9:   recaps published by Orienteering USA (orienteeringusa.org).\n10: - The construct: a race is a sequence of controls you navigate between; your **split time**\n11:   is the time between two consecutive controls, and the central post-race question is\n12:   \"where did I lose time?\" The artifact every event already produces is a **result/split\n13:   file**: the **IOF XML 3.0** `ResultList` (with split times per control) and the\n14:   Sport Software CSV / WinSplits SPL formats every timing system exports. Eventor's own API\n15:   returns `IOF XML 3.0 ResultList` with `includeSplitTimes`.\n16: - The recurring need: after every race (dozens of local clubs worldwide run events weekly),\n17:   orienteers upload splits to Attackpoint and analyze the same thing, by hand or in\n18:   WinSplits — where they lost time per leg. WinSplits even computes it for them: \"time\n19:   without mistakes\" and per-leg \"time loss\", coloring mistake legs pink. World of O News\n20:   hand-draws \"graphical split-time analysis\" plots for every championship. A shareable,\n21:   deterministic card of one racer's splits is the thing people read and would post.\n22: - Evidence:\n23:   - https://attackpoint.org/ — hub: \"High-Performance Training Logs for Orienteers\", \"Race\n24:     performance analysis\", and a \"Recent Splits\" list of freshly-uploaded races.\n25:   - http://obasen.orientering.se/winsplits/online/en/help_table.asp?databaseId=101857&categoryId=0 —\n26:     WinSplits help: \"For each runner, position, name, club, finish time, time behind the\n27:     winner and times and positions at each control are shown.\" and \"Some split times in the\n28:     list have pink background colour. This means that the runner's time loss on such a leg is\n29:     relatively large. In other words: a mistake has probably been made.\"\n30:   - https://orienteering.sport/iof/it/data-standard-3-0 — the IOF Interface Standard 3.0 XML\n31:     schema, the interchange format for \"result lists\" with \"Improved support and\n32:     documentation for split times\"; times as ISO 8601 and total seconds.\n33:   - https://news.worldofo.com/2026/07/11/woc-2026-knock-out-sprint-maps-and-results —\n34:     World of O News publishes hand-made \"graphical split-time analysis\" plots per WOC race.\n35:   - https://orienteeringusa.org/2026/04/junior-nationals-and-flying-pig-xxviii-event-recap/ —\n36:     every OUSA event recap links \"Splits on Attackpoint\" and WinSplits as the canonical\n37:     post-race artifact.\n38: - Why m0saic fits: the card is rectangles on a canvas — one bar per control (split time vs\n39:   the best leg, time-lost legs highlighted pink), the header block (name, club, class,\n40:   course, finish time, place, time behind), and a footer. Props mirror the IOF XML fields\n41:   verbatim (splits array in seconds, timesBehind, club, className). Batchable (one card per\n42:   racer per race), fully deterministic, no media needed — pure numbers.\n43: - Risks: split arrays are long (10–25 controls) so layout needs a scroll/compact mode and a\n44:   split-count cap; \"time loss\" is computed by WinSplits from the whole class, so the template\n45:   should take loss-per-leg as a prop rather than re-derive it; niche is small and the\n46:   community is older — but it is exactly the \"community organised around one construct with a\n47:   shared tool\" the brief asks for.\n48: \n49: ### 2. Scuba dive-log recap card\n50: - Who: Scuba divers, on divelogs.org, scubaboard, and Subsurface users. Log every dive in a\n51:   computer/app (Subsurface, MacDive, Shearwater).\n52: - The construct: the dive log; the artifact is **UDDF/UDCF XML** or Subsurface's `.ssrf` —\n53:   per-dive date, site, max depth, duration, temp, buddy, and a depth profile.\n54: - The recurring need: after a trip, a \"dive #100\" milestone or a trip recap card; people\n55:   share these by hand (Instagram \"100th dive\" posts are common).\n56: - Evidence: https://divemates.app/help/export-from-another-app (\"Subsurface's own `.ssrf`\n57:   file works just as well\"); https://github.com/ericgriffin/submersion (\"full UDDF 3.2 import\n58:   and export\"); https://www.divelogs.org/ (\"Get comprehensive statistics\").\n59: - Why m0saic fits: numbers-only card — dive count, max depth, total bottom time, depth\n60:   profile as a bar/line.\n61: - Risks: the profile chart is the hard, taste-heavy part; milestones are one-off rather than\n62:   weekly, so less batchable than a race recap.\n63: \n64: ### 3. Fantasy football weekly recap image\n65: - Who: Fantasy football commissioners (ESPN/Sleeper/Yahoo leagues), r/fantasyfootball and\n66:   league group chats.\n67: - The recurring need: a weekly recap/trash-talk image; Sleeper already ships a \"Weekly\n68:   Report with Awards\" image every Tuesday, and commissioners still hand-make their own.\n69: - Evidence: https://support.sleeper.com/en/articles/3204499-league-history-and-weekly-reports;\n70:   https://www.tfofantasy.com/blog/ai-fantasy-football-recaps (\"nobody wants to write the\n71:   weekly recap\").\n72: - Why m0saic fits: matchup/standings are structured props; batchable one card per matchup.\n73: - Risks: crowded commercial space (ffwrapped, Fantasy Genius, NextTeamUp, Sleeper's built-in\n74:   report), and it skews to prose more than rectangles; a mainstream candidate that lost on\n75:   \"someone already sells exactly this\".\n76: \n77: ### 4. MMO raid parse card (Warcraft Logs / FFLogs)\n78: - Who: WoW/FFXIV raiders, on Warcraft Logs and FFLogs.\n79: - The recurring need: a shareable \"I parsed 99\" / guild progress card after raid night.\n80: - Evidence: https://wipefest.gg/; https://www.warcraftinsights.com/; https://thegamercodex.com/en/final-fantasy-xiv/tools/fflogs.\n81: - Why m0saic fits: parse percentile, DPS, kill time, rank are clean props.\n82: - Risks: Warcraft Logs/FFLogs already generate embeddable signature/parse images, and this is\n83:   close in shape to day 026's osu! scorepost card; not enough of a gap.\n84: \n85: ## Pick\n86: Orienteering race split-analysis card. It is the one genuinely fresh niche construct left on\n87: the founder's list that this repo has not touched, it has a real machine-readable artifact\n88: (IOF XML 3.0 with split times) whose field names a template can mirror, and a sharp recurring\n89: moment (after every race, everyone asks \"where did I lose time?\" — a chart people currently\n90: make by hand). It beats fantasy football because no commercial tool already owns it, and it\n91: beats scuba because the moment recurs weekly instead of once per milestone.\n92: \n93: ## Rejected today\n94: - Scuba dive-log recap: milestone-based, less batchable, profile chart is taste-heavy.\n95: - Fantasy football recap: crowded commercial space (Sleeper ships its own image report).\n96: - MMO raid parse card: logs sites already embed parse images; too close to osu scorepost.\n\n(End of file - total 96 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Scout — 2026-10-24\n\n## Candidates (4, best first)\n\n### 1. Orienteering race split-analysis card\n- Who: Orienteers. They gather on **Attackpoint** (https://attackpoint